{"code":0,"data":{"title":"Playing with a Self-Hosted GitLab Community Edition","content":"# Playing with a Self-Hosted GitLab Community Edition\n\nThis self-hosted setup uses Docker containers for both `gitlab-ce` and `gitlab-runner`.\n\n## What is GitLab?[ #](#what-is-gitlab)\n\n**GitLab** is a source-code repository management system (an open-source project). It uses Git for version control and ships a web UI. Many teams with relatively simple org structures prefer it as their Git platform.\n\n## What can it do?[ #](#what-can-it-do)\n\nAs a repository manager, GitLab gives a friendlier UI for managing project code. It can also integrate CI/CD: deploy **GitLab Runner** for continuous integration and delivery.\n\nThat is enough for everyday project work.\n\n> Large, complex programs often separate the repository system from CI/CD. Repo tooling varies (homegrown or based on other OSS); CI/CD is often Jenkins, with a richer mature plugin and pipeline ecosystem. GitLab may not fit every such case.\n\n## Setup steps[ #](#setup-steps)\n\nHere is how I stood up a test instance.\n\n### Stand up the GitLab core[ #](#stand-up-the-gitlab-core)\n\nDocker Hub already publishes **GitLab** images, so deploying with Docker is straightforward.\n\n![docker-gitlab-ce](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/530bd021d4e1.png)\n\nThe image has many tags — pin a version or take the latest.\n\nI deploy the management UI with a `docker-compose.yml`. When adding the service, set `GITLAB_OMNIBUS_CONFIG` so startup options are passed into the container.\n\nOnce the service is up, open a browser to your self-hosted GitLab:\n\n![login](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/e2e1519d6a0e.png)\n\n### Sign in as admin[ #](#sign-in-as-admin)\n\nNext, sign in as admin to finish configuration. The username is `root`; the password comes from inside the container. You can also grab the container IP for later settings:\n\n![gitlab-ce-info](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/9442444b2afa.png)\n\nUse that password to log in.\n\n![admin-dashboard](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/1c87025556eb.png)\n\nAfter login you land in admin settings, where you can configure the whole repository system.\n\n> We are not building GitLab only as a code host. If you only need a repo, consider something like `gogs` — fast, simple, and GitHub-like. We want CI/CD as well.\n\n#### CI/CD environment[ #](#cicd-environment)\n\nThis is the main part.\n\nWith the above in place, deploy **GitLab Runner** — the CI/CD agent. Under admin, open **Runners** to see the available list:\n\n![runners](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/f6e0f2e2f440.png)\n\nIt is empty. Normally you install GitLab Runner on a worker machine.\n\nI only wanted to poke at GitLab features, so I kept it simple: run **GitLab Runner** in Docker and register it locally. For IP resolution across containers, I added a hosts entry:\n\nvbnetCopy\n\n```vbnet\n192.168.176.2 gitlab.me\n```\n\n`192.168.176.2` may differ on your machine — use the GitLab container’s IP so other containers resolve correctly.\n\nRegister the runner (copy the `token` from **Registration token** in the screenshot above):\n\n![runner-register](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/53a51f2560c9.png)\n\nAfter registration, the runner appears in the Runners list.\n\n> I chose the `docker` executor so I can build with a Docker image (gcc). That fits a Docker-based deploy. If Runner is installed on a bare machine that already has a toolchain, use the `shell` executor. If the worker must SSH to another host to build, use `ssh`. Pick based on your environment.\n\nAfter registration, the new runner shows up:\n\n![runner-list](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/d954e9363c1c.png)\n\nClick the left-hand link for details:\n\n![runner-details](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/1e90dd45eba5.png)\n\nClick the pencil on the right to edit settings:\n\n![runner-enable-untag](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/5329eafbdd66.png)\n\nI checked **Run untagged jobs** and saved.\n\nThat finishes the basic Runner setup.\n\n> Runners have more options — see the official docs.\n\n### Create a regular user[ #](#create-a-regular-user)\n\nOpen a private window, go to login, click **Register now**, fill in the form, then have an admin approve the user. Once approved, the new user can sign in (the screenshot shows a user who already created a project):\n\n![user-projects](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/279ae5d2774b.png)\n\nThis account is the developer used for the CI/CD test below.\n\n#### Create a project[ #](#create-a-project)\n\nClick **New project** and fill in the basics:\n\n![new-project](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/e89af7df63df.png)\n\n#### Set up `.gitlab-ci.yml`[ #](#set-up-gitlab-ciyml)\n\nAdd a `.gitlab-ci.yml` at the repo root with pipeline jobs.\n\nMine is minimal: `script` for the build command, `only` for the branch (`main`), and `tags` matching the runner’s tags.\n\n> More `.gitlab-ci.yml` detail: [official docs](https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/yaml/gitlab_ci_yaml.html).\n\n#### Push code and run CI[ #](#push-code-and-run-ci)\n\nWith everything ready, write code and push to `main`:\n\n![git-push](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/fc979b40e6df.png)\n\nRight after the push, the pipeline updates:\n\n![pipelines](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/94be700a77c7.png)\n\nOpen the running pipeline for job details:\n\n![pipeline-success](/post/deploygitlabcerunner/121b5fa098bf.png)\n\nThat completes a basic GitLab setup with CI/CD. Overall it is a practical code-management stack.\n\n## Concrete configs[ #](#concrete-configs)\n\nTwo test configs for reference.\n\n### My test configs[ #](#my-test-configs)\n\n`.gitlab-ci.yml`:\n\nyamlCopy\n\n```yaml\nstages:\n  - build\n\nbuild source:\n  stage: build\n  tags:\n    - linux\n  script:\n    - gcc -o main main.c\n  only:\n    - main\n  interruptible: true\n```\n\n`docker-compose.yml`:\n\nyamlCopy\n\n```yaml\nversion: \"3.0\"\n\nservices:\n  gitlab-web:\n    image: gitlab/gitlab-ce\n    restart: always\n    hostname: 'gitlab.me'\n    environment:\n      GITLAB_OMNIBUS_CONFIG: |\n        external_url 'http://gitlab.me:2080/'\n    ports:\n      - '2080:2080'\n      - '2443:443'\n    volumes:\n      - ./gitlab/config:/etc/gitlab\n      - ./gitlab/logs:/var/log/gitlab\n      - ./gitlab/data:/var/opt/gitlab\n    networks:\n      - gitlab\n\n  gitlab-runner:\n    image: gitlab/gitlab-runner:alpine\n    restart: always\n    depends_on:\n      - gitlab-web\n    volumes:\n      - /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock\n      - ./gitlab/gitlab-runner:/etc/gitlab-runner\n    networks:\n      - gitlab\n\nnetworks:\n  gitlab:\n    name: gitlab-network\n```\n\n### Pitfalls I hit[ #](#pitfalls-i-hit)\n\n> Likely gotchas:\n>\n> 1. If `external_url` in `GITLAB_OMNIBUS_CONFIG` includes a port, Docker’s service listen binds to that port.\n> 2. In the runner `config.toml`, under `runner.docker`, set `network_mode` to `gitlab-network` to match `docker-compose.yml`.\n\n## Links[ #](#links)\n\n[how-to-install-gitlab-using-docker-compose](https://www.czerniga.it/2021/11/14/how-to-install-gitlab-using-docker-compose/)\n","locale":"en","slug":"deploygitlabcerunner"}}